      "completion_tokens": 1036,
      "output": "\n\nWater is a chemical substance composed of two hydrogen atoms and one oxygen atom, forming the molecule **H₂O**. It is a fundamental and ubiquitous substance on Earth, essential for all known forms of life. Here's a breakdown of its key characteristics:\n\n### **Chemical Composition**\n- **Molecular Structure**: Water is a polar molecule, with oxygen (more electronegative) pulling electrons away from hydrogen, creating partial negative and positive charges. This polarity leads to **hydrogen bonding**, which gives water its unique properties.\n\n### **Physical Properties**\n- **States of Matter**: Water exists as a **liquid** at standard temperature and pressure (0–100°C), but it can also be a **solid** (ice) at 0°C or below and a **gas** (water vapor) at 100°C or above.\n- **Unique Properties**: \n  - **High Specific Heat**: It absorbs and releases heat slowly, stabilizing temperatures in organisms and environments.\n  - **Cohesion & Adhesion**: Molecules stick to each other (cohesion) and to other surfaces (adhesion), enabling processes like capillary action in plants.\n  - **Solvent Ability**: It dissolves many substances, earning it the title \"universal solvent.\"\n\n### **Biological Importance**\n- **Life-Sustaining**: Water is a primary component of cells, blood, and bodily fluids. It facilitates nutrient transport, waste removal, and biochemical reactions.\n- **Metabolic Reactions**: It acts as a reactant or product in processes like photosynthesis and cellular respiration.\n\n### **Environmental Role**\n- **Hydrological Cycle**: Water cycles through evaporation, condensation, precipitation, and runoff, shaping Earth's climate and ecosystems.\n- **Erosion & Landscaping**: It carves rivers, lakes, and canyons over time, influencing geological features.\n\n### **Other Key Traits**\n- **Tasteless, Odorless, and Colorless**: In small quantities, but appears blue in large volumes due to light absorption.\n- **Abundance**: Covers ~71% of Earth's surface, though only ~2.5% is freshwater.\n\nIn summary, water is a simple yet extraordinary molecule whose unique properties make it indispensable for life, environmental stability, and countless natural and industrial processes."
